WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.390
Last year I built a model to

2
00:00:02.390 --> 00:00:04.030
predict which pupils would pass.

3
00:00:04.730 --> 00:00:06.450
On last year's pupils it

4
00:00:06.450 --> 00:00:08.030
was right every time.

5
00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:10.620
This year it was wrong for

6
00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:11.840
one pupil in three.

7
00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:15.530
Today: why we test a model on

8
00:00:15.530 --> 00:00:17.110
data it has never seen.

9
00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:19.360
The fix is simple.

10
00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:22.660
Split your data before you start: about

11
00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:25.280
eighty percent to train the model, and

12
00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:27.540
twenty percent locked away to test it.

13
00:00:28.090 --> 00:00:29.870
The test set plays the part

14
00:00:29.870 --> 00:00:31.130
of next year's pupils.

15
00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:35.020
A model can remember its training pupils

16
00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:37.000
without learning anything useful.

17
00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:40.350
The test pupils are new to it, so its

18
00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:42.870
score on them is an honest guess of

19
00:00:42.870 --> 00:00:44.090
how it will do next year.

20
00:00:45.290 --> 00:00:47.670
Here is the test error as we make the

21
00:00:47.670 --> 00:00:49.270
model more and more complex.

22
00:00:50.110 --> 00:00:53.270
It falls at first, then rises again.

23
00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:55.500
The middle model is the best

24
00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:56.480
one for new pupils.

25
00:00:57.860 --> 00:01:00.200
A model that bends to fit every

26
00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:02.600
training pupil is overfitting.

27
00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:06.100
It learns the noise, like one pupil's

28
00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:08.280
lucky day, as if it were a rule.

29
00:01:08.990 --> 00:01:10.730
It looks perfect in training

30
00:01:10.730 --> 00:01:12.790
and fails on new data.

31
00:01:14.070 --> 00:01:16.590
One rule matters most.

32
00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:19.340
Never peek at the test set

33
00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:20.800
while you build the model.

34
00:01:21.390 --> 00:01:24.290
If you tune the model to the test

35
00:01:24.290 --> 00:01:27.190
pupils, the test stops being fair.

36
00:01:28.490 --> 00:01:29.070
Quick check!

37
00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:32.460
A model scores one hundred percent

38
00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:35.540
on its training pupils and sixty

39
00:01:35.540 --> 00:01:37.460
percent on the test pupils.

40
00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:41.160
Which score tells you how

41
00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:42.560
it will do next year?

42
00:01:43.850 --> 00:01:46.250
The test score, sixty percent.

43
00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:49.320
The perfect training score only shows

44
00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:51.140
the model remembered its pupils.

45
00:01:51.980 --> 00:01:54.440
The big gap is a sign of overfitting.

46
00:01:55.650 --> 00:01:58.150
In the app, open the overfitting lab.

47
00:01:58.770 --> 00:02:01.110
Raise the complexity slider and

48
00:02:01.110 --> 00:02:03.510
watch the training error fall while

49
00:02:03.510 --> 00:02:05.350
the test error turns back up.

50
00:02:06.550 --> 00:02:07.090
Your turn.

51
00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:10.560
Why do we keep twenty percent of the data

52
00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:12.660
away from the model while we build it?

53
00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:16.300
To check it on pupils it has never seen.

54
00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:19.980
Remember: train on most of the data, test

55
00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:22.960
once on the rest, and a big gap between

56
00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:25.040
the two scores means overfitting.

57
00:02:26.270 --> 00:02:28.770
Try it yourself: make a rule from ten

58
00:02:28.770 --> 00:02:31.730
cards, then test it on five hidden cards.

59
00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:34.120
Did your rule still work on the

60
00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:35.400
cards it had never seen?
